<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Lima (1929)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1929">1929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, signed on 3 June 1929 with the mediation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, settled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacna">Tacna</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arica">Arica</a> dispute left by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a>. Tacna was returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and Arica remained with Chile, which paid Peru compensation and agreed to provide port facilities. The settlement ended nearly half a century of tension between the two countries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lima-1929.jpg" alt="Treaty of Lima (1929)" width="320" height="311" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Janitoalevic · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tacna-Arica_Dispute_(1883-1929).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
